# Epics

An epic is a large body of work under a program initiative, typically
one to three months long. This team's Product Owner owns it. The epics
here are the ones this team owns alone; an epic that spans teams lives in
the system repository. An epic is not a unit of work: the stories under
it are planned into sprints, and its progress is the share of their story
points done.

## Documents here

- Name: `epNNN-short-name.md`, numbered in order and never reused.
- Format: an SRD in the pinned parent release's Markdown format, with the fields docs/constitutions/design.md lists for this level.
- Status: `to_be_agreed` while it is drafted, `agreed` once it passes review, and `retired` when it is replaced. Only agreed documents are planned and rolled up. A retired document keeps its path, because links pin it.
- Parent: an initiative in the system repository, named by URL. Features and stories name an epic as theirs.
- Changes go through an issue and the corpus gate, never a direct edit on main.

## What reads them

- tpm plan candidates lists the stories under an epic in the team's Product Backlog.
- Features and stories name an epic as their parent; its progress is the story points of its done stories over those of all its agreed stories.

See tpm help backlog for the whole hierarchy.
